{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AzpHK5Yj22Qt5BLwKHf93E","type":"tool_result","content":"6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) must re-verify that no code changed since `Plan.Review.CommitSHA` before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\n\n**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n\n```\n\nExecutor failure rate across the gate:\n\n```bash\nsqlite3 ~/.ard/state/albertcbrown/ard/_controller.db \"\n  SELECT printf('%d failed / %d jobs = %.1f%%', SUM(status = 'failed'), COUNT(*),\n                100.0 * SUM(status = 'failed') / COUNT(*))\n  FROM jobs WHERE stage != 'scanner' AND status != 'running';\"\n```\n\nA usage-limit stop (`limit_stop = 1`) is not an executor failure; list them\nseparately with `SELECT id, issue, fail_reason FROM jobs WHERE limit_stop = 1`.\n\nPer-issue table, kept in the go/no-go comment:\n\n| Issue | PR | Jobs | Failed | Cost (USD) | Human code edits | Merged | Notes |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba70a651-ea4a-4861-a970-006d1e1f371d","uuid":"5614d2c1-e468-4212-88b8-76f3f1d8bf44","timestamp":"2026-11-23T14:26:54.333Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"6. **A moved head is not a pass.** Just before close-out the loop compares the branch with the pinned commit. If it gained code outside `.ard/<issue#>/` meanwhile, a `changes` verdict is still posted and labelled (it is the conservative one), but an `approve` is posted without `:done`: the request label stays and the review runs again on the new head. The reviewed commit is recorded in the close-out record so the merger (#46) must re-verify that no code changed since `Plan.Review.CommitSHA` before merging: a small window remains between the head check and close-out adding `:done`. A review closed out by the Reaper after a restart has no head information, so an approve is treated as stale.\n\n**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n\n```\n\nExecutor failure rate across the gate:\n\n```bash\nsqlite3 ~/.ard/state/albertcbrown/ard/_controller.db \"\n  SELECT printf('%d failed / %d jobs = %.1f%%', SUM(status = 'failed'), COUNT(*),\n                100.0 * SUM(status = 'failed') / COUNT(*))\n  FROM jobs WHERE stage != 'scanner' AND status != 'running';\"\n```\n\nA usage-limit stop (`limit_stop = 1`) is not an executor failure; list them\nseparately with `SELECT id, issue, fail_reason FROM jobs WHERE limit_stop = 1`.\n\nPer-issue table, kept in the go/no-go comment:\n\n| Issue | PR | Jobs | Failed | Cost (USD) | Human code edits | Merged | Notes |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AzpHK5Yj22Qt5BLwKHf93E","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChL1rsZ48CG6HimxFqhtC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017SNvZ7CCeE3Zi9rqQ9J6wL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/proposal.md'\ns=open(p).read()\nanchor=\"an approve is treated as stale.\\n\"\ni=s.index(anchor, s.index(\"6. **A moved head is not a pass.**\"))+len(anchor)\nnew=\"\"\"7. **A gone PR is stale, not failed.** A human may merge or close the PR, or GitHub may delete its branch, while a review runs. At close-out the controller reads the PR before committing. If it was merged or closed, or the committer reports the branch gone, the job ends `stale`: nothing is committed to the branch, only `:running` is removed, there is no `ard:executor:failed` and no failure comment, and `review.pr_gone` is logged with the state (`merged`, `closed` or `branch_gone`). The PR review is still posted, best-effort: if posting fails the error is logged and the job still closes. A failed PR read falls through to the commit, where a missing branch gives the same outcome. Gate 2's failure rate counts only `failed` jobs, so these are not executor failures. A worker has no equivalent: its PR is a draft that cannot be merged while it runs, so a branch deleted mid-job stays a real failure.\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:i]+new+s[i:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\np='docs/runbooks/gate-2.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=\"A usage-limit stop (`limit_stop = 1`)\"\ns=s.replace(a,\"A review whose PR was merged, closed or branch-deleted before close-out ends\\n`stale`, not `failed`, so the query above does not count it.\\n\\n\"+a,1)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngit -c core.excludesFile=/dev/null status --porcelain --untracked-files=all; go test ./... 2>&1 | grep -v \"^ok\\|no test files\" | tail"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1281,"cache_read_input_tokens":61413,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1281},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba70a651-ea4a-4861-a970-006d1e1f371d","uuid":"4f8fb383-8150-4c4c-a527-5b94874398f2","timestamp":"2026-11-23T14:26:59.793Z","request_id":"req_011ChL1rsR7ntu8Zsm5gRn4L"}
